This asserts that there is some element in the array list `arrList` that has the value `x`. `IndexOf(x)` returns the index of the first occurrence of `x` in the list, so if the return value is non-negative, it implies that `x` is present in the list.
--------------------Here's the corresponding C# code that uses `Debug.Assert` with comments following the "@@@" template:

```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0); //@@@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array 

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
```
The `Debug.Assert` statement in the code above checks if the return value of `arrList.IndexOf(x)` is non-negative (greater than or equal to 0), which means `x` is contained in the original `arrList`. This assertion essentially validates the "PUT_IndexOfContract" method.
--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0);

// @@@ Old and new array list lengths are equal after performing operation
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);

// @@@ Old and new index of x values are equal
Debug.Assert(Old_x == New_Ret);

// @@@ Old and new of arrList's last index of x values are equal
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

// @@@ If old arrList contains x, new arrList also contains x
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);

// @@@ If the last index of x in old array list is greater than or equal to 0, 
// @@@ then it should also be greater or equal to new array list's index of x (assuming index starts from 0)
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_Ret);
```
Note that the last debug assert is a bit tricky since index comparison can be different in different programming languages depending on how indexing starts. To be able to make a generalized statement about this, we'll need to know the nature of the IndexOf method in the ArrayList class.
--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0, "If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array");
```

Just a reminder that this assertion assumes that `arrList.IndexOf(x)` will return a non-negative integer when `x` is found in the list. `IndexOf` method will return the zero-based index of the first occurrence of the element within the list if it is found; otherwise, it returns -1. Therefore, the assertion checks that the returned value is non-negative.
--------------------Here is the C# code using `Debug.Assert()` corresponding to the comments:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0, "If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array");
```

This `Debug.Assert()` will only be executed if the condition in the first argument of `Debug.Assert()` is `false`. In this case, the condition checks that the return value of `arrList.IndexOf(x)` is non-negative, which means that x is in the old array if the index is non-negative.
--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0);

// @@@ Old array list count is greater or equal to new array list count 
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount >= New_arrListCount);

// @@@ The old X is still in the list after we've called IndexOf
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX);

// @@@ If x was in the list before, LastIndexOf should point to it
if (Old_arrListContainsX)
    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount - 1);

// @@@ If x was added, LastIndexOf for x should be equal to or greater than before
if (Old_arrListContainsX && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX < New_arrListCount - 1)
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX + 1);

// @@@ If x is inserted in the array at the tail, LastIndexOf should point to it
if (Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount && Old_arrListContainsX)
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount - 1);

// @@@ IndexOf should return -1 if x was not in the list before
if (!Old_arrListContainsX)
    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1);

```
